.TH DEVTOOL 1 "2026-07-14" "devtool 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
devtool \- transactional multi-project build, test, validation, and artifact tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B devtool
.RI [ global-options ] command " [command-options]"
.PP
.B devtool
.RI [ global-options ] "apply-overlay ARTIFACT" " [apply-options]"
.PP
.B devtool
.RI [ global-options ] "test" " [selection-options]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B devtool
provides one runner-independent interface for inspecting, restoring, building, testing,
linting, packaging, cleaning, and validating .NET, Android, Gradle/JVM, Python, and
explicit command projects.
.PP
It can apply overlays and snapshots transactionally, validate them with the selected
project runner, create commits, preserve structured evidence, publish live progress to
desktop or Android frontends, and roll back touched repository paths after failure.
.PP
One mutating run is allowed per repository. A second mutating invocation fails while the
current run owns the repository lock.
.SH PROJECT TYPES
.TP
.B dotnet
Uses discovered or configured solutions/projects. Validation normally runs restore,
build, and test.
.TP
.B android
Uses Gradle plus Android project inspection, local JVM tests, Android Lint, Android
artifact verification, and optional physical-device instrumentation.
.TP
.B gradle
Uses Gradle for JVM projects with task discovery, structured task progress, JUnit result
collection, lint/static-analysis tasks, and artifact discovery.
.TP
.B python
Supports uv, Poetry, PDM, Pipenv, Hatch, and isolated venv/pip workflows, plus pytest,
unittest, tox, nox, configured linters/type checkers, PEP 517 builds, and package checks.
.TP
.B command
Runs explicitly configured restore, build, test, lint, package, clean, and validation
commands.
.PP
Use
.BR --project-type
when automatic detection is ambiguous. An explicit target type in
.I .devtool.toml
takes precedence over detection.
.SH PRIMARY COMMANDS
.TP
.B restore " | " rs
Prepare the toolchain and restore or synchronize dependencies.
.TP
.B build " | " b
Build the selected target.
.TP
.B test " | " t
Run all configured tests or a selected subset. See
.B TEST SELECTION.
.TP
.B lint
Run configured lint and static-analysis tools.
.TP
.B validate " | " v
Run the runner-specific validation pipeline. Also accepts the optional artifact scopes
.B overlay
and
.B overlays.
.TP
.B validation-menu " | " vm
Open the validation-only interactive cockpit. The equivalent nested command is
.BR "menu validation" .
Use
.B --wizard
to enter the guided flow directly,
.B --no-fzf
to force the numbered fallback,
.B --runtime-tasks
to query the complete Gradle task catalog,
.BI "--recipe " NAME
to open a built-in or saved recipe,
.BI "--artifact " PATH
or
.BI "--matrix-values " VALUES
to expand recipe placeholders, and
.B --print-command
to emit the compiled canonical command without running it.
.TP
.B start-validation " | " sv
Create a persistent sequential validation session.
.TP
.B resume-validation " | " rv
Resume a persistent validation session.
.TP
.B validation-status " | " vs
Show the active session without mutating or recovering it.
.TP
.B validation-plan " | " vplan
Show the durable phase graph, dependency metadata, checkpoint requirements, and current verdicts.
.TP
.B validation-events " | " vevents
Read append-only validation events, optionally after an event cursor.
.TP
.B validation-report " | " vreport
Show or generate the immutable durable validation report for a session.
.TP
.B validation-pause " | " vpause
Request a pause after the currently running phase. This control does not wait for the repository run lock.
.TP
.B validation-stop " | " vstop
Request immediate termination of the active phase while preserving it as retryable.
.TP
.B validation-reset " | " vreset
Reset one phase and its graph dependents with
.BR --phase " or " --from-phase ;
use
.B --all
to reset all verdicts while retaining attempt evidence.
.TP
.B validation-accept-plan-change " | " vapc
Accept an incompatible validation graph change with a required
.BR --reason .
.TP
.B validation-recover " | " vrec
Recover a provably dead phase owner or resume an incomplete rollback.
.TP
.B validation-abort " | " va
Abort and roll back a session, or retain applied changes with
.BR --keep-changes .
.TP
.B validate-all
Run the runner-specific validation pipeline and continue through independent phases
after failures so the final summary includes every captured error. Use
.B --fail-fast
to stop at the first failed phase.
.TP
.B wine-validate " | " wv
Run validation with the configured Wine environment.
.TP
.B run " | " rn
Run the configured application or module.
.TP
.B package " | " pkg
Create configured distributable artifacts.
.TP
.B clean " | " cl
Remove runner-owned generated output.
.TP
.B inspect " | " info
Inspect project detection, selected runner, toolchain, variants/tasks, tests, and expected
artifacts.
.TP
.B status " | " st
Show resolved repository, target, runner, execution environment, and configuration status.
.TP
.B doctor " | " dr
Diagnose devtool and project prerequisites. Use
.B --fix
to create safe devtool state/cache directories where supported. Use
.B --explain
on native Termux to include provider selection and rejection reasons.
.SH ARTIFACT COMMANDS
.TP
.B apply-overlay " | " ao " ARTIFACT"
Transactionally apply one overlay archive.
.TP
.B apply-overlays " | " aos " ARTIFACT..."
Apply an overlay bundle or multiple ordered overlays transactionally.
.TP
.B apply-snapshot " | " as " ARTIFACT"
Apply a repository snapshot transactionally.
.TP
.B apply overlay|overlays|snapshot
Nested equivalents of the three apply commands.
.TP
.B validate-overlay " | " vo " ARTIFACT"
Validate artifact structure and metadata without applying it.
.TP
.B validate-overlays " | " vos " ARTIFACT..."
Validate a bundle or an ordered set of overlays. Use
.B --fail-on-conflict
to reject duplicate writes.
.TP
.B inspect-artifact " | " ia " ARTIFACT"
Print artifact, bundle, inventory, validation, and optional repository-diff metadata.
.TP
.B bundle overlays " OVERLAY... -o OUTPUT"
Create an overlay bundle with optional repository identity, target mapping, validation
policy, embedded validator, exact validation.tasks defaults, and final commit metadata.
Use
.BR --validation-task
or
.BR --validation-tasks
to write Gradle task defaults into the bundle manifest.
.SH OTHER COMMANDS
.TP
.B batch " STEP..."
Run ordered operations such as
.B validate
or
.B validate-all
or
.BR apply-overlay=/path/file.zip .
.TP
.B commit " | " ci " MESSAGE"
Create a Git commit.
.TP
.B tag " NAME"
Create a Git tag; use
.BR -m
for an annotated tag message.
.TP
.B release-notes
Generate notes between
.B --from-ref
and
.BR --to-ref .
.TP
.B init-config " | " init
Create a starter
.I .devtool.toml.
.TP
.B config path|show|defaults|init|edit|resolve|doctor
Inspect, create, edit, resolve, and diagnose repository or global configuration. Use
.B --global
with
.BR path ,
.BR show ,
.BR defaults ,
.BR init ,
.BR edit ,
and
.B doctor
for the sparse user config at
.IR ~/.config/devtool/config.toml .
.TP
.B termux doctor|packages|explain
Inspect native Termux runtime and provider readiness, print read-only package recommendations, or explain provider selection/rejection decisions.
.B termux doctor
accepts
.B --distro NAME
to probe the installed PRoot guest's own Python/Java/Gradle/CMake/Ninja/clang/Git commands and
.B --explain
to include provider reasoning. The doctor reports per-tool Termux Android providers, Android SDK/full-NDK readiness when applicable, and local SSH/chrootctl/tmux/PRoot availability. The dedicated Termux diagnostics support
.B --format text|json
and never install or upgrade Termux packages; OpenJDK suggestions are resolved read-only from installed/available package names before using a conservative fallback.
.TP
.B detect
Detect project type; use
.B --format text|json.
.TP
.B targets " | " tg
List named targets.
.TP
.B logs
Show the latest saved command log; use
.B --print
to emit it.
.TP
.B history
List structured run history.
.TP
.B rollback
Rollback a commit or use
.B --last.
.TP
.B handoff
Generate a repository handoff document.
.TP
.B ai-prompt " | " ap
Generate an implementation prompt with project, artifact, target, and commit metadata.
.TP
.B completion zsh
Print the current zsh completion definition.
.TP
.B control-center " | " gui
Launch the Qt 6 Devtool Control Center.
.TP
.B ipc-server
Run or start the local Control Center IPC broker.
.SH GLOBAL PROJECT OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -r , " --repo" " PATH"
Repository root.
.TP
.BR -c , " --config" " FILE"
Configuration file path.
.TP
.BR --target " NAME"
Named target from
.I .devtool.toml.
Targets are opt-in and are never selected merely because a default target exists.
.TP
.BR --variant " NAME"
Build/test variant within the selected target.
.TP
.BR --solution " PATH"
Exact solution path for restore/build/test.
.TP
.BR --project " PATH"
Exact project path for run or validation.
.TP
.BR --project-type " TYPE"
Override detection. TYPE is
.BR auto ,
.BR dotnet ,
.BR android ,
.BR gradle ,
.BR python ,
.BR command ,
or
.BR unknown .
.SH GLOBAL EXECUTION OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Stream full child-process output.
.TP
.BR -y , " --yes"
Assume yes for confirmations.
.TP
.BR -f , " --force"
Allow explicitly forced dangerous operations.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Print resolved commands without executing them.
.TP
.BR -k , " --continue-on-error"
Continue with the next independent step after an error.
.TP
.B --fail-fast
Stop a batch at the first failed step.
.TP
.B --performance
Write a JSON performance report.
.TP
.BR --performance-file " FILE"
Select the performance report path.
.SH OUTPUT AND CLIPBOARD
.TP
.BR --machine-file " FILE"
Save the machine envelope.
.TP
.BR -p , " --copy"
Copy a concise success summary or verbose failure output.
.TP
.B --no-clipboard
Disable clipboard prompts and copy behavior.
.TP
.BR --copy-via " BACKEND"
Prefer
.BR auto ,
.BR socket ,
.BR fifo ,
.BR termux ,
.BR x11 ,
or
.BR wayland .
.TP
.BR --theme " auto|plain"
Select the summary theme.
.TP
.BR --stdout-payload " auto|summary|raw"
Select standard-output content.

.SH GLOBAL CONFIGURATION
Devtool supports a sparse user preference file at
.IR ~/.config/devtool/config.toml .
It is merged as defaults only: built-in defaults, then global user preferences, then the repository
.I .devtool.toml,
then explicit CLI flags. The global file is intentionally not a shadow repository config. Keep
project kind, targets, tasks, test profiles, artifact paths, and validation filters in
.IR .devtool.toml .
.PP
Useful global sections are
.BR [cli.output] ,
.BR [cli.hud] ,
.BR [cli.apply] ,
.BR [cli.environment] ,
.BR [environment] ,
.BR [termux_chroot] ,
.BR [android] ,
and
.BR [dotnet] .
Run
.B devtool config init --global --minimal
to create a starter file,
.B devtool config edit --global
to open it in an editor, and
.B devtool config doctor --global
to warn about repo-scoped or command-scoped keys placed globally. Editor selection prefers
.B DEVTOOL_EDITOR,
then
.BR VISUAL ,
.BR EDITOR ,
then
.BR micro .
.PP
On Android/Termux,
.B [termux_chroot] project_kinds
controls which project types auto mode may route through the configured chroot. The default remains
.BR ["android","gradle"] ,
but users may add values such as
.B python
or use
.B all
when the chroot should own every supported project kind.

.SH EXECUTION ENVIRONMENTS
.TP
.B --host
Force command phases to run in devtool's current environment.
.TP
.BR --chroot " NAME"
Run command phases in a named chroot through
.BR "sudo chrootctl --name NAME exec -- zsh -lc" .
.TP
.BR --execution-environment " BACKEND"
Select
.BR auto ,
.BR native-termux ,
.BR chroot ,
.BR proot-distro ,
.BR desktop-linux ,
.BR android-device ,
.BR external-device ,
or
.BR ssh .
.TP
.BR --ssh " HOST"
Run the entire devtool invocation on an SSH host inside tmux. SSH is transport-only; local paths are not translated into remote paths.
.TP
.BR --ssh-port " PORT"
Set the SSH port. The default is 22 unless overridden by configuration.
.TP
.BR --ssh-user " USER"
Set the SSH login user.
.TP
.BR --ssh-identity " PATH"
Use a local SSH identity file.
.TP
.BR --ssh-session " NAME"
Set the remote tmux session name.
.TP
.BR --ssh-cwd " PATH"
Start the remote devtool process in this remote working directory. If omitted, use the SSH login default working directory; no local path is inferred.
.TP
.BR --ssh-tool " COMMAND"
Set the remote devtool executable.
.TP
.B --ssh-detached
Start or reuse the remote tmux session without attaching. An attached session may be detached with Ctrl-b followed by d.
.TP
.BR --proot-distro " DISTRO"
Keep devtool orchestration on the host and run planned external command phases inside the named PRoot-Distro container. The guest does not need devtool installed. The current repository is automatically bound to /workspace/project unless disabled or overridden.
.TP
.BR --proot-user " USER"
Select the guest user.
.TP
.BR --proot-cwd " PATH"
Set the absolute initial working directory inside the guest.
.TP
.BR --proot-repo-mount " PATH"
Set the absolute guest mount point used by automatic repository binding.
.TP
.BR --proot-bind " HOST:GUEST"
Add a host-to-guest bind. Repeatable. Explicit guest destinations must be absolute.
.TP
.BR --proot-env " NAME=VALUE"
Pass an explicit guest environment variable. Repeatable. Host HOME, PATH, JAVA_HOME, Gradle home, Android SDK/NDK homes, and backend-specific devtool variables are not blindly inherited; devtool-managed execution/backend variables are reserved and cannot be overridden.
.TP
.B --proot-no-auto-bind-repo
Disable automatic binding of the current repository.
.TP
.B --proot-shared-home
Request PRoot-Distro shared-home behavior.
.TP
.B --proot-shared-tmp
Share Termux temporary storage with the guest. This option is Termux-only.
.TP
.B --proot-isolated
Use PRoot-Distro isolated mode. Termux-only and mutually exclusive with --proot-minimal.
.TP
.B --proot-minimal
Use PRoot-Distro minimal mode. Termux-only and mutually exclusive with --proot-isolated.
.TP
.BR --proot-stage-dir " PATH"
Set the host helper staging directory bound into the guest. Staging is cleaned after the command plan finishes.
.TP
.BR --termux-resource-profile " PROFILE"
Choose auto/automatic, conservative, balanced, or performance defaults for workers, JVM memory, memory guard, and native parallelism. Explicit user resource settings remain authoritative. Phone-local defaults are not injected into independent SSH/remote execution plans.
.TP
.B --termux-wake-lock
Acquire a Termux wake lock while command phases run and release it on success, failure, or interruption.
.TP
.B --no-termux-wake-lock
Disable wake-lock integration.
.TP
.BR --shared-storage-policy " POLICY"
Set allow, warn, or deny behavior when a native Termux repository is located on Android shared storage.
.TP
.B --allow-shared-storage-build
Override the shared-storage guard for one run.
.TP
.BR --artifact-export " DIR"
Copy verified Android/Gradle artifacts to a final destination such as /sdcard/Download after private-storage verification.
.TP
.B --no-auto-chroot
Do not automatically route incompatible Termux operations into a chroot.
.TP
.B --no-bootstrap-downloads
Never download or install missing toolchain components during Devtool-managed Android/Gradle bootstrap.
.TP
.B --deny-network
Deny Devtool-managed bootstrap network downloads for this run.
.TP
.B --offline
Alias for
.BR --no-bootstrap-downloads
and
.BR --deny-network ,
and also passes Gradle offline mode for Android/Gradle runs.
.TP
.BR --toolchain-profile " NAME"
Use a named
.BR [chroots.NAME]
toolchain profile from global or repository config.
.TP
.BR --android-sdk-root " PATH"
Set the Android SDK root for Android/Gradle runs.
.TP
.BR --gradle-command " PATH"
Set the Gradle or gradlew executable for Android/Gradle runs.
.TP
.BR --gradle-provider " PROVIDER"
Select the Gradle provider for Android/Gradle runs: \fBauto\fR, \fBwrapper\fR, \fBtermux\fR, \fBsystem\fR, or \fBdownload\fR. A complete project Wrapper remains preferred in auto mode.
.TP
.BR --java-home " PATH"
Set the JDK home for Android/Gradle runs.
.TP
.BR --java-provider " PROVIDER"
Select the Java provider for Android/Gradle runs: \fBauto\fR, \fBtermux\fR, or \fBsystem\fR. An explicit \fB--java-home\fR remains authoritative.
.TP
.BR --gradle-user-home " PATH"
Set
.B GRADLE_USER_HOME
for Android/Gradle runs.
.TP
.BR --native-provider " PROVIDER"
Select native compiler capability provider: \fBauto\fR, \fBtermux-sysroot\fR, \fBndk\fR, or \fBsystem\fR. Android external native builds still require a complete NDK.
.TP
.BR --native-cache " PROVIDER"
Select native compiler cache: \fBauto\fR, \fBccache\fR, or \fBoff\fR.
.TP
.BR --android-build-tools-provider " PROVIDER"
Select native Android SDK build-tools provider: \fBauto\fR, \fBtermux\fR, \fBsdk\fR, \fBcommit451\fR, or \fBnone\fR. Native Termux/Bionic requires runnable Termux helpers; Commit451 linux-glibc-arm64 binaries are used only on compatible Linux/chroot hosts.
.TP
.BR --aapt2-provider " PROVIDER"
Select AAPT2 provider: \fBauto\fR, \fBtermux\fR, \fBsdk\fR, \fBrevanced\fR, or \fBnone\fR. Native Termux AAPT2 is capability-probed against the selected Android platform before use.
.PP
Devtool records the selected environment in structured logs and HUD state. Chroot workers
use a recursion guard, translate known Termux/chroot paths, and verify the mapped project
before execution.
.SH TEST SELECTION
The same selection model is translated to .NET filters, Gradle/JUnit filters, Android
instrumentation arguments, Python framework arguments, and native filters for other
supported runners where possible. Selector flags such as
.BR --class ,
.BR --filter ,
and
.B --tag
can be used on
.BR validate
and on
.BR apply-overlay --validate .
For validation commands, however,
.B --task
is an exclusive native validation-unit plan. Android/Gradle targets still treat values as
Gradle task names, so only the named Gradle task or tasks are placed in the validation
pipeline. For Python/Node/Rust and other non-Gradle runners, --task selects native
validation units such as test, lint, build, package-check, or runner-specific test targets.
Configured preflight, dependency, compile, package, lint, finalize, and embedded
artifact-validator phases are not added unless selected explicitly.
.TP
.B --list-tests " | " --list
Discover and list tests without executing them.
.TP
.BR --profile " NAME"
Use a saved test profile.
.TP
.BR --select " VALUE"
Select by type, suite, tag, class, or name. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR --id " TEST-ID"
Select an exact framework test ID. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR --tag " TAG"
Include a tag, marker, or trait. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR --exclude-tag " TAG"
Exclude a tag, marker, or trait. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR --path " PATH"
Select tests below a source path. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR --class " CLASS"
Select a test class. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR --method " METHOD"
Select a test method. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR --filter " EXPRESSION"
Pass a framework-native filter.
.TP
.BR --task " TASK"
On
.BR test ,
override the Gradle test task. On validation and artifact-apply validation, replace the
entire built-in validation pipeline with this task. Repeat to run multiple
explicit tasks in one native Gradle invocation. Artifact manifests may declare the same
exclusive task plan with
.B validation.tasks
or
.B validation.gradle_tasks ;
CLI flags take precedence, so
.B --task
and
.B --test-task
replace manifest tasks.
.TP
.BR --test-task " TASK"
Alias for
.BR --task .
.TP
.B --failed
Rerun tests that failed in the previous structured run.
.TP
.B --changed
Select tests related to changed files.
.TP
.BR --changed-from " REF"
Choose the Git base for
.BR --changed .
.TP
.B --instrumented
Run Android instrumentation tests on a device.
.TP
.B --no-build
For .NET tests, use already-built output.
.PP
Reports keep separate discovered, selected, executed, passed, failed, skipped, and excluded
counts. Notifications show the selected total rather than the complete catalog total.
.SH INTERACTIVE VALIDATION COCKPIT
Run
.B devtool menu validation
or
.B devtool vm
to open the validation-only terminal interface. The cockpit uses fzf when
available and bat for rich previews, with a numbered terminal fallback when
fzf is unavailable or
.B --no-fzf
is supplied.
.PP
The interface reads the stable revisioned validation status and plan APIs. It
compiles selections into the normal Devtool CLI and executes that argv directly;
it does not implement a separate runner. Session mutations and continuation commands include the current revision,
preventing a stale menu or notification from changing or advancing a newer
state. Ctrl-R and the numbered Refresh action reload the resolved repository
context, session revision, plan, events, and task catalog.
.PP
The state-aware landing screen exposes next, through, selected, remaining,
retry, safe retry, pause, stop, recover, reset, plan acceptance, finalization,
reporting, and explicit keep-or-rollback abort actions. The guided wizard covers
one-shot, collect-all, durable sequential, watch, matrix, Wine, artifact
integrity, bundle integrity, and apply-time validation. The final review can run,
dry-run, copy, edit, or save the generated command as a global or repository
recipe.
.PP
Common keys are Enter to select, Tab for multi-selection, Ctrl-R to refresh,
Ctrl-P to toggle previews, Ctrl-D for dry-run, Ctrl-Y to copy, Ctrl-E to edit,
Ctrl-S to save a recipe, Ctrl-L to open the latest log, and Escape to return.
Saved recipes preserve explicit global invocation context and are read from
.I ~/.config/devtool/validation-recipes.toml
and
.IR REPOSITORY/.devtool/validation-recipes.toml .
.SH VALIDATION
.TP
.B --skip-restore
Skip dependency restore.
.TP
.B --skip-build
Skip the build phase.
.TP
.B --skip-test
Skip tests.
.TP
.BR --test-profile " NAME"
Use a saved validation test profile.
.TP
.B --watch
Rerun validation when files change.
.TP
.BR --watch-interval " SECONDS"
Set the watch polling interval.
.TP
.BR --matrix " VALUES"
Validate comma-separated runtime IDs, target names, or variants.
.TP
.BR --matrix-mode " rid|target|variant"
Interpret matrix values as runtime IDs, named targets, or build/test variants.
.TP
.BR --max-workers " COUNT"
Override the validation worker count for this invocation.
.TP
.BR --cpu-limit " COUNT"
Override the validation CPU budget for this invocation.
.TP
.BR --gradle-heap-mb " MIB"
Override the Gradle validation heap for this invocation.
.TP
.BR --memory-guard-mb " MIB"
Override the free-memory guard for this invocation. Zero disables the guard.
.TP
.BR --low-priority " | " --normal-priority
Select low or normal scheduler priority for validation child processes.
.TP
.BR --parallel " | " --no-parallel
Enable or disable Gradle parallel execution for validation.
.TP
.BR --build-cache " | " --no-build-cache
Enable or disable the Gradle build cache for validation.
.TP
.BR --daemon " | " --no-daemon
Allow or disable Gradle daemons for validation.
.PP
Use
.B --resource-profile
for validation resources and
.B --test-profile
for test selection. The validation
.B --profile
alias is deprecated and emits a warning.
.PP
These one-run overrides are applied after
.B --resource-profile
and therefore take precedence without modifying
.IR .devtool.toml .
.SS Durable Android/Gradle validation
.TP
.BR --phase " PHASE"
Run the named phase and every unsatisfied dependency.
.TP
.BR --phase-only " PHASE"
Run exactly one phase only when its prerequisites are already complete.
.TP
.BR --through " PHASE"
Run every eligible phase through the selected checkpoint.
.TP
.B --complete
Run all remaining required phases.
.TP
.BR --from-phase " PHASE"
Start at a phase whose prerequisites are already complete.
.TP
.BR --max-infrastructure-retries " COUNT"
Bound automatic environment/tooling retries per phase.
.TP
.BR --max-resource-retries " COUNT"
Bound corroborated resource retries per phase. Resource retries may use the Android low-power profile.
.TP
.B --no-auto-retry
Disable automatic environment and resource retries.
.TP
.BR --max-runtime " DURATION"
Supervise the active child and pause when the invocation budget is reached.
.TP
.BR --stop-below-battery " PERCENT"
Pause before or during a phase when the Android battery is too low.
.TP
.B --require-charging
Pause unless the Android device is charging.
.TP
.BR --stop-above-temperature " CELSIUS"
Pause before or during a phase when the device exceeds the thermal limit.
.PP
Durable sessions store an explicit dependency graph and independent configuration, source, resource, and test fingerprints. A successful command is discarded as stale if relevant inputs change while it runs. Artifact minimum phases are enforced at creation and finalization. Unsafe graph drift requires
.B validation-accept-plan-change --reason
or a session restart.
.SS Stable validation control API
Schema-v4 sessions expose API-v1 JSON envelopes for Android and automation clients. Use nested forms such as:
.PP
.nf
  devtool validate status --json
  devtool validate plan --json
  devtool validate events --after-event EVENT_ID --json
  devtool validate report --json
  devtool validate run --scope next --if-revision REV --json
  devtool validate run --scope through --through package --if-revision REV --json
  devtool validate run --scope selected --phase compile --phase lint --if-revision REV --json
  devtool validate run --scope remaining --if-revision REV --json
  devtool validate pause --after-phase --if-revision REV --json
  devtool validate pause --cancel --if-revision REV --json
  devtool validate stop --if-revision REV --json
.fi
.TP
.BR --session " ID"
Address a specific durable validation session instead of the active session.
.TP
.BR --if-revision " REV"
Apply a mutation only when the durable session revision matches. Accepted controls advance the revision; liveness heartbeats do not.
.TP
.BR --scope " next|through|selected|remaining"
Choose the stable run scope. Selected phases include unmet dependency closure.
.TP
.B --json
Emit one API-v1 validation-control envelope.
.TP
.BR --machine " pretty|compact"
Emit the same envelope using pretty or compact JSON formatting.
.TP
.BR --machine-file " PATH"
Mirror the exact JSON envelope to a file without adding status prose to stdout.
.TP
.BR --after-event " EVENT_ID"
Return durable events after a previously received cursor.
.TP
.BR --event-limit " COUNT"
Bound event records included with status.
.PP
Status, plan, and event reads are non-mutating. Pause and stop publish atomic out-of-band requests while the validator owns the repository lock. Machine abort requires an explicit
.B --keep-changes
or
.B --rollback
choice.
.PP
Normal .NET validation is optimized as:
.PP
.nf
  dotnet restore TARGET
  dotnet build --no-restore TARGET
  dotnet test --no-build TARGET
.fi
.PP
Recognizable explicit
.B dotnet build
and
.B dotnet test
commands receive the same optimization during validation. Arbitrary wrapper scripts are
left unchanged. A standalone
.B devtool test
still builds unless
.B --no-build
is explicitly requested.
.PP
Android validation prepares the toolchain, configures Gradle, compiles, runs local unit
tests, runs Android Lint, packages artifacts, verifies them, and optionally runs physical
device instrumentation according to target policy.
.PP
Python validation respects the existing environment manager and lock file, then performs
the configured dependency synchronization, tests, lint/type checks, PEP 517 build, package
verification, and optional clean-environment import/install checks. Devtool-owned Python
venvs, caches, and environment snapshots are stored under devtool XDG directories unless
an explicit Python environment path is configured.
.SH ARTIFACT VALIDATION MANIFESTS
Schema-v2 overlay and overlay-bundle manifests may declare validation defaults in a
.B validation
object. The canonical Gradle task field is
.B validation.tasks ;
accepted aliases are
.BR validation.gradle_tasks ,
.BR validation.test_tasks ,
.BR validation.test_task ,
and
.BR validation.task .
When tasks are present, devtool uses the exclusive Gradle task pipeline: only those
tasks are run, with normal artifact checksum, inventory, and transactional apply checks
still enforced. CLI flags take precedence: 
.B --no-validate
disables manifest validation and tasks,
.B --validate
enables validation even when the manifest says disabled, and
.BR --task / --test-task
replace manifest task lists. Overlay bundles can set bundle-wide tasks in
.B validation.tasks
or per-overlay tasks on an
.B apply_order
entry through
.BR validation.tasks .
.SH APPLY OPTIONS
.TP
.B --validate
Run target validation after applying. This CLI flag takes precedence over manifest
validation mode values that disable validation.
.TP
.B --no-validate
Disable validation requested by artifact metadata and ignore manifest validation.tasks.
.TP
.B --validate-only
Do not apply; only run the selected validation.
.TP
.B --preview
Preview archive changes.
.TP
.B --diff
Show a repository-relative unified diff.
.TP
.B --interactive
Show the preview and request confirmation.
.TP
.B --no-manifest-check
Disable devtool artifact manifest validation.
.TP
.B --ignore-checksum
Skip SHA-256 presence, format, and mismatch checks while retaining repository identity, path safety, inventory completeness, payload size, conflict, and validation-policy checks.
.TP
.B --keep-artifact
Keep the source artifact and sidecars after a successful apply.
.TP
.B --delete-artifact-on-failure
Explicitly delete a failed source artifact. By default failed artifacts are preserved for
retry and diagnosis.
.TP
.B --no-rollback-on-failure
Disable rollback when an immediate apply or its non-resumable validation fails.
.TP
.BR --failure-action " pause|rollback|keep|prompt"
Choose the result of a deferred or sequential validation failure. The default is
.B pause,
which preserves both the applied tree and rollback point. Use
.B rollback
for immediate verified restoration or
.B keep
to close the transaction while retaining changes without a successful verdict.
.TP
.BR --commit " MESSAGE"
Commit after successful apply and optional validation.
.TP
.B --no-commit
Disable a commit requested by artifact metadata.
.TP
.B --no-artifact-script
Skip embedded artifact validation scripts while retaining built-in validation.
.PP
The validation skip flags,
.BR --test-profile ,
and test selection flags such as
.BR --class ,
.BR --filter ,
and
.B --tag
also apply to artifact validation. Supplying
.B --task
instead creates an exclusive validation plan and suppresses all other validation phases,
including embedded artifact validation scripts. Gradle targets use Gradle task names;
non-Gradle targets use their native validation actions or test targets. Artifact manifest,
checksum, inventory, and transactional apply safety checks still run before mutation.
.SH TRANSACTIONAL APPLY AND EVIDENCE
Before mutation, devtool backs up paths touched by the artifact. Immediate apply failures
retain the traditional rollback behavior. Deferred and sequential validation failures block
and preserve the applied tree plus rollback point unless an explicit rollback policy is
selected. Commit failure after successful validation leaves the session validated but
uncommitted.
.PP
Rollback uses a per-path journal. Existing content is restored through staged, hash-verified,
atomic replacements; newly added paths are quarantined and verified absent. Interrupted
rollback remains active and can be resumed with
.B devtool validate recover.
Status, checkpoint, and validation dry-run operations do not mutate durable session state.
.PP
Validation logs, machine reports, test results, lint diagnostics, and artifact evidence are
stored outside the repository before rollback. Generated build/cache directories are not
transaction content. Failed input artifacts are preserved by default.
.PP
Runner cleanup is performed in a finalization step. .NET invokes
.BR "dotnet build-server shutdown" ;
Gradle stops scoped daemons; Python runner-created subprocesses are terminated.
.SH RESUMABLE VALIDATION
Deferred and sequential validation is persisted as a dependency graph. Each phase records
its prerequisite keys, required/optional status, scope, input domains, and attempt history.
Selecting a phase with
.B --phase
includes every unmet prerequisite. Selecting it with
.B --phase-only
refuses to run when a prerequisite is not already complete.
.PP
Use
.B devtool validate reset --from-phase PHASE
to reset a phase and all graph dependents while retaining attempt evidence, or
.B devtool validate reset --all
to reset every verdict. Unsafe plan drift, including removal of a required phase, phase
reordering, or identity/scope changes, enters recovery-required state. Review the new plan
and use
.B devtool validate accept-plan-change --reason REASON
for an audited acceptance, or restart the session. The equivalent top-level controls are
.B validation-reset
and
.B validation-accept-plan-change.
.PP
The artifact
.B validation.minimum_phase
checkpoint is enforced when the session is created and when it is finalized. Configuration,
source, resource, and test inputs are fingerprinted independently. A phase result is discarded
as stale when one of its relevant input domains changes while that phase is running.
.PP
Validation attempts distinguish code and test failures from user interruption, timeout,
toolchain gaps, environment loss, and corroborated resource pressure. Active phases publish
heartbeats and enforce runtime, battery, charging, and thermal guards while the child process
is running. Exit 137 without supporting resource evidence is treated as an environment
interruption rather than being labeled out-of-memory.
.PP
.B --max-infrastructure-retries COUNT
and
.B --max-resource-retries COUNT
bound automatic retries per phase. Corroborated resource failures may retry with the
.B android-low-power
profile. Use
.B --no-auto-retry
for manual-only retry control.
.SH ANDROID AND GRADLE
The Android runner integrates the same backend used by the
.B build-apk
compatibility command. It supports JDK and SDK inspection/setup, complete Gradle Wrapper
preference, Termux/system/download Gradle providers, Termux/system Java providers,
ARM-compatible AAPT2 handling, Build Tools/platform/NDK/CMake discovery, Termux-native
execution, and chroot fallback. In auto mode an incomplete project Wrapper is reported as
an error instead of silently changing Gradle versions.
.PP
Gradle is launched with a bundled init script that emits ordered run-scoped events. Terminal,
desktop, and Android views consume the same task and test stream. Stale, wrong-run, invalid,
or out-of-order events are rejected.
.PP
JUnit XML and Android Lint XML/SARIF provide authoritative test and diagnostic totals.
APK, AAB, AAR, JAR, WAR, and ZIP outputs can be checked for existence, freshness, integrity,
metadata, signatures where available, and SHA-256.
.PP
With instrumentation enabled, devtool can build and install app/test APKs, invoke
AndroidJUnitRunner on the current rooted device or an external ADB device, collect results,
and select instrumentation classes or methods.
.SH PYTHON
The Python runner detects existing uv, Poetry, PDM, Pipenv, Hatch, or venv/pip projects and
does not silently replace the selected manager. It supports pytest, unittest, tox, and nox,
plus configured Ruff, Black, isort, mypy, Pyright, Pylint, and Bandit checks.
.PP
PEP 517 wheels and source distributions can be structurally verified, checked with Twine,
and installed into a clean temporary environment for import and entry-point smoke tests.
.SH HUD AND ANDROID APP BRIDGE
.TP
.BR --auto-hud , " --hud-auto"
Start or reuse the selected live HUD.
.TP
.B --no-hud
Do not publish live HUD progress.
.TP
.BR --hud-mode " MODE"
Select desktop, android-bridge, desktop-window, gtk-window, web, studio, terminal, or another
supported renderer.
.TP
.B --hud-overlay
Also request the user-enabled Android floating overlay.
.TP
.B --hud-no-overlay
Keep the run in the Android app without opening the floating overlay.
.TP
.B hud
Run a HUD renderer directly. It supports state directory, mode, host, port, poll interval,
recent-event count, one-shot, active-run following, and overlay controls.
.TP
.B android-hud-bridge " | " termux-hud-bridge
Forward shared Termux/chroot state to the Android app. It supports active-run following and
explicit overlay presentation.
.PP
App-launched commands record launch acceptance, Termux shell startup, authenticated bridge
connection, runner/toolchain stages, progress, result delivery, and exit status. Foreground
Termux mode can expose the real shell output while the same run continues updating the app.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
Install the packaged zsh completion as
.I _devtool
in a directory from
.BR $fpath ,
or print it with:
.PP
.B devtool completion zsh
.PP
The completion covers all commands and aliases, global execution/output/HUD options,
selective tests, validation profiles, Android/Gradle/Python project types, artifact apply and
bundle operations, Control Center commands, and configuration-derived target/profile names.
.SH RELEASE QUALIFICATION
The source repository includes portable and native release gates. Run:
.PP
.B python3 tools/release-qualify.py --core-only
.PP
for Python tests, source compilation, shell validation, version consistency, and a deterministic
source archive. On release hosts, use
.B --require-qt
and
.B --require-android
to convert unavailable native toolchains into failures, and
.B --device-tests
to run connected Android instrumentation. The canonical cross-package versions are stored in
.I release/versions.json .
.PP
Android release builds require explicit
.B DEVTOOL_RELEASE_STORE_FILE ,
.B DEVTOOL_RELEASE_STORE_PASSWORD ,
.B DEVTOOL_RELEASE_KEY_ALIAS ,
and
.B DEVTOOL_RELEASE_KEY_PASSWORD
values. Unsigned release APKs are rejected and verified with apksigner.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .devtool.toml
Per-repository target, runner, task, artifact, test-profile, validation, environment, and
execution configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/devtool/config.toml
Sparse user preferences for output, HUD, apply policy, default chroot, and Android/Termux chroot project-kind policy.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/devtool/logs
Saved human-readable command logs.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/devtool
Structured run state, evidence, performance reports, and transaction metadata.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/devtool/android
Staged Android build backend and related shared resources.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/devtool-android-hud
Default shared Android bridge state on Termux.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DEVTOOL_GLOBAL_CONFIG
Override the user global config path.
.TP
.B DEVTOOL_EDITOR
Preferred editor for
.B devtool config edit
commands.
.TP
.B DEVTOOL_XDG_DIR
Override devtool runtime state storage.
.TP
.B DEVTOOL_RUN_ID
Run identity shared by the CLI, Gradle event stream, Android app, and desktop HUD.
.TP
.B DEVTOOL_EXECUTION_CONTEXT
Marks delegated execution, including chroot workers, and prevents recursive delegation.
.TP
.B DEVTOOL_HUD_SINGLETON
Controls HUD process reuse. The value
.B kill
replaces an older renderer when explicitly used.
.TP
.B DEVTOOL_PROJECT_KIND
Override detected project kind through environment configuration.
.SH REMOVED OPTIONS
The former standalone notification, Tasker, and multi-run lease options are not accepted:
.BR --notify ,
.BR --notify-progress ,
.BR --tasker ,
.BR --tasker-progress ,
.BR --tasker-broadcast ,
.BR --concurrency ,
.BR --lock-timeout ,
and
.BR --no-wait .
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success or an accepted validation-control request. For the stable validation control API, 2 means invalid request, 3 stale session revision, 4 a blocked validation session, 5 recovery required, and 6 control unavailable in the current state. Other non-zero values indicate command failure, invalid configuration/artifact, unsupported execution environment, interruption, or rollback/recovery that requires the requested operation to be rerun.
